  m_text: Sea turtles are ancient, majestic marine reptiles that have navigated the
    world's oceans for millions of years. Unlike their land-dwelling cousins, they
    feature streamlined bodies and large, powerful flippers that make them highly
    adapted for a life at sea, though this means they cannot retract their heads
    or limbs into their shells for protection. Found in all oceans except the polar
    regions, these remarkable creatures have varied diets depending on their species,
    feeding on anything from seagrass and sponges to jellyfish. They are perhaps
    most famous for their extraordinary navigational skills, with females migrating
    thousands of miles across open seas to return to the exact same beaches where
    they were born to lay their eggs. Today, however, nearly all species of sea turtles
    are classified as endangered or vulnerable, facing severe threats from habitat
    destruction, climate change, and ocean plastic pollution.
